WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.560
Film Courage: Does the screenwriter find themselves
an agent or does the work find them the agent?

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.669
Lee Jessup: In a perfect world, the way that
we’d love to see it work is your script

00:00:09.669 --> 00:00:12.669
comes in as a finalist for the Nicholl [Fellowship].

00:00:12.669 --> 00:00:15.259
Every manager and agent in town reaches out
to you.

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:16.259
Boom!

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:18.460
You have a manager or an agent, right?

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:19.990
You win the UCLA contest.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.090
Very much the same scenario.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.650
Austin Film Festival, etc.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.560
That is not always the scenario.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.380
The scenario for most writers is they are
going to work very hard to find a rep.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.669
It’s usually going to be a manager first.

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:35.710
Because a manager really today serves as talent
scouts for the big agencies.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.300
And it’s going to take a lot of work, a
lot of diligence and a lot of consistency

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.590
to find that rep.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:45.609
So (for example) one of my writers who just
got signed a couple of months ago had this

00:00:45.609 --> 00:00:50.520
group of managers and agents that she would
approach every time she would finish a script.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.559
And it was vetted and she knew it was ready
to go.

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:57.890
She had placed in the top 3 in Final Draft
some years ago but nothing materialized rep

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:58.890
wise.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.390
And so she had finished a new script she was
really excited about and we were sitting across

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.150
from one another and going through her list
and she was like [makes a sound of exasperation]

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.730
“Do I have to send a script to this manager
again?!

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:11.140
I’ve sent him like 5 scripts and he doesn’t
like anything.”

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.140
And I said “Yeah.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.140
You send it to everybody.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.500
That’s just what we do.”

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.049
And three weeks later that manager become
her manager who is now sending her out on

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:21.680
an insane amount of general and show runner
meetings.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.001
Because finally the thing came aboard because
he was like “I knew you were a writer.

00:01:26.001 --> 00:01:27.001
I knew you were good.

00:01:27.001 --> 00:01:28.001
I know that you’re consistent.

00:01:28.001 --> 00:01:30.300
Now you wrote something great.”

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.490
So oftentimes it ends up being the writer
finding the rep, rather than the rep finding

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.970
the writer.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:41.259
Even though we’d like to think that in a
perfect world a rep would come knocking on

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:42.259
your door.

00:01:42.259 --> 00:01:44.560
In truth, it’s a referral business.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.320
So the best reps and the best relationships
are forged through referrals.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.200
Where somebody will say “Hey, my friend
Joe just finished this great script.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.399
You should read it.”

00:01:56.399 --> 00:01:57.820
Because really that’s what the industry
trusts.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:59.630
We’re a highly social industry.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:05.340
So if you can get those referrals which is
why you foster relationships, those are priceless.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.720
It’s much easier to get a rep through a
referral than a contest placement unless you’re

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.660
winning that contest.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:19.100
Or through the Blacklist, unless you get the
coveted 9 rated on the Blacklist.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.361
So really it’s about working those relationships
so those referrals will be in place when you

00:02:23.361 --> 00:02:24.450
have that script that’s ready to share.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:24.950
Question for the Viewers: Without naming names,
share how a writer you know found an agent.

